{%- comment -%}
  Mirrors ../design-system/src/react/ReviewCard/ReviewCard.tsx: a star rating, a quote, and an
  attribution line (name, optional location, optional avatar), inside a card.

  This is a snippet, not a block, because two callers need the same card: blocks/mionas-review-card
  .liquid renders it from theme-editor settings, and sections/mionas-google-reviews.liquid renders
  it from metaobjects synced out of the Google Business Profile API. Defining the markup twice would
  let the two drift, and the contract test could only ever guard one of them.

  Accepts:
    rating              1-5. Defaults to 5.
    show_rating         Pass false to hide the star row entirely. Anything else (including nil)
                        shows it, so existing callers that never pass it are unaffected. This is an
                        explicit `== false` test rather than `| default: true`, because Liquid's
                        default filter treats false as empty and would flip it straight back to true.
    quote               Review text. Omitted renders no quote.
    clamp_lines         Number of lines to collapse the quote to. 0 or omitted means no clamp,
                        no trigger and no dialog -- the shape every caller had before "Leer más"
                        existed. Any other value clamps with CSS (the full text stays in the DOM,
                        unlike a Liquid `truncate`) and emits the dialog below.
    dialog_label        Accessible name for the dialog, e.g. "Reseña de Marta G.". The trigger's
                        name is this prefixed with "Leer más — ", so a screen reader crossing six
                        cards does not meet six identical "Leer más" buttons. Only read when
                        clamp_lines > 0.
    name                Attribution name. Omitted renders no attribution.
    location            Optional. Shown after the name.
    avatar              Optional image. Not used by the Google-sourced caller.
    avatar_alt          Optional. Falls back to name.
    shopify_attributes  Theme-editor attributes, passed by the block caller only.

  Every part is optional on purpose: .review-card__content is a flex column with a gap, so an
  element that renders empty still consumes a gap. Each part is therefore wrapped in its own guard
  rather than emitted and hidden with CSS.

  The star row lives in snippets/mionas-review-stars.liquid -- the "Leer más" dialog below renders
  the same row, and one copy is better than two. Every colour here comes straight from
  --review-card-* tokens
  (assets/design-tokens.css, synced from ../design-system/dist/tokens.flat.css) -- this
  component has no untokenized brand colour to declare locally.
  The outer card look (background/border/radius/shadow) spends --card-base-* the same way, but NOT
  --card-base-padding: ReviewCard.tsx explicitly overrides Card's own bottom padding to 0 and pads
  uniformly via an inner Box p="lg" instead (see ReviewCard.module.css's comment on cascade order),
  so this snippet does the same with --space-lg on .review-card__content rather than the card token.

  Card.module.css no longer caps width -- the design system pushed that cap down into the individual
  components that want one (ProductCard, ProductPromoCard); ReviewCard.tsx deliberately has none. This
  snippet mirrors that: it declares no width limit and fills whatever container it is placed in (a
  grid column, a carousel slide, and so on).

  The clamp, the "Leer más" trigger and the dialog are theme-only: ReviewCard.tsx has no clamp,
  and the design system has no Dialog component to mirror at all. Do not read them as drift when
  syncing -- they are additions on top of the synced component, not a diverged copy of it. If the
  design system ever grows its own clamped ReviewCard, revisit this to follow it.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% liquid
  assign card_rating = rating | default: 5
  assign card_avatar_alt = avatar_alt | default: name

  assign render_rating = true
  if show_rating == false
    assign render_rating = false
  endif

  # The clamp/dialog pair is opt-in. A caller that passes no clamp_lines -- blocks/mionas-review
  # -card.liquid, whose quotes are merchant-authored and short -- renders exactly what it rendered
  # before this feature existed, with no dialog-component wrapper and no extra DOM.
  assign clamp = clamp_lines | default: 0
  assign render_clamp = false
  if clamp > 0 and quote != blank
    assign render_clamp = true
  endif

  assign card_dialog_label = dialog_label | default: 'Reseña completa'
%}

{%- comment -%}
  The wrapper is opened and closed with conditional tags rather than always emitted, because
  DialogComponent declares `requiredRefs = ['dialog']` (assets/dialog.js) and would log a missing
  -ref error for an unclamped card, which has no dialog inside it.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if render_clamp -%}
  <dialog-component class="review-card-dialog">
{%- endif -%}

<div class="review-card" {{ shopify_attributes }}>
  <div class="review-card__content">
    {%- if render_rating -%}
      {%- render 'mionas-review-stars', rating: card_rating -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if quote != blank -%}
      {%- if render_clamp -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          mionas-review-clamp replaces the bare <p> as the flex child of .review-card__content, so
          the trigger can be hidden without leaving a gap behind: the content column is a gapped
          flex column, and a direct child that renders empty still consumes a gap.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <mionas-review-clamp
          class="review-card__clamp"
          style="--review-card-quote-clamp: {{ clamp }};"
        >
          <p class="review-card__quote review-card__quote--clamped">&ldquo;{{ quote }}&rdquo;</p>
          {%- comment -%}
            The explicit `dialog-component/` prefix, not the bare `/showDialog` form
            blocks/popup-link.liquid uses: assets/component.js resolves a bare binding to the
            closest ancestor that is a Component *or* whose tag ends in `-component`, and this
            button sits inside <mionas-review-clamp>. The bare form would work only by virtue of
            that element's name. The prefixed form resolves with element.closest('dialog-component'),
            which is scoped to the card the click landed in -- load-bearing when carousel_on_mobile
            renders every card's HTML twice.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <button
            type="button"
            class="button-unstyled review-card__more"
            on:click="dialog-component/showDialog"
            aria-label="Leer más — {{ card_dialog_label }}"
          >
            Leer más
          </button>
        </mionas-review-clamp>
      {%- else -%}
        <p class="review-card__quote">&ldquo;{{ quote }}&rdquo;</p>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if name != blank or avatar != blank -%}
      <div class="review-card__attribution">
        {%- if avatar != blank -%}
          {{
            avatar
            | image_url: width: 80
            | image_tag: width: 40, height: 40, class: 'review-card__avatar', alt: card_avatar_alt
          }}
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- if name != blank -%}
          <p class="review-card__name">
            {%- if location != blank -%}
              {{- name -}}, {{ location }}
            {%- else -%}
              {{- name -}}
            {%- endif -%}
          </p>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</div>

{%- if render_clamp -%}
  <dialog
    ref="dialog"
    class="review-card-dialog__panel dialog-modal"
    scroll-lock
    aria-label="{{ card_dialog_label }}"
  >
    {%- comment -%}
      tabindex="-1" + autofocus put initial focus on the review text rather than the close button.
      showModal() otherwise focuses the first focusable descendant -- the close button -- which
      then renders with the browser's focus ring the instant the dialog opens, reading as a stray
      circle around the X. autofocus must sit on a descendant: Chrome ignores it on the <dialog>
      element itself and falls back to the first focusable child regardless.

      This removes the ring without deleting any focus indicator. Tabbing still reaches the close
      button and still shows its ring, which is what keyboard users need. It also means a screen
      reader opens on the review itself instead of announcing "Close, button".
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="review-card-dialog__body" tabindex="-1" autofocus>
      {%- if render_rating -%}
        {%- render 'mionas-review-stars', rating: card_rating -%}
      {%- endif -%}
      <p class="review-card__quote">&ldquo;{{ quote }}&rdquo;</p>
      {%- if name != blank -%}
        <p class="review-card__name">
          {%- if location != blank -%}
            {{- name -}}, {{ location }}
          {%- else -%}
            {{- name -}}
          {%- endif -%}
        </p>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
    <button
      ref="closeButton"
      type="button"
      class="button button-unstyled close-button review-card-dialog__close"
      on:click="dialog-component/closeDialog"
      aria-label="{{ 'accessibility.close_dialog' | t }}"
    >
      {{- 'icon-close.svg' | inline_asset_content -}}
    </button>
  </dialog>
  </dialog-component>
{%- endif -%}

{% stylesheet %}
  .review-card {
    background: var(--card-base-background);
    border-radius: var(--card-base-radius);
    border: var(--card-base-border-width) solid var(--card-base-border-color);
    box-shadow: var(--card-base-shadow);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .review-card__content {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    padding: var(--space-lg);
  }

  /* Text's "body" role sets size/line-height/weight elsewhere in the theme; only what differs
     (colour, italic) is set here, same convention blocks/mionas-text.liquid follows. */
  .review-card__quote {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-family-archivo);
    font-size: var(--text-role-body-font-size);
    line-height: var(--text-role-body-line-height);
    font-weight: var(--text-role-body-font-weight);
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--review-card-quote-foreground);
  }

  .review-card__attribution {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
  }

  .review-card__avatar {
    width: var(--review-card-avatar-size);
    height: var(--review-card-avatar-size);
    border-radius: var(--review-card-avatar-radius);
    object-fit: cover;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  /* Text's "label" role sets size/line-height/weight/tracking elsewhere; only colour differs. */
  .review-card__name {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-family-archivo);
    font-size: var(--text-role-label-font-size);
    line-height: var(--text-role-label-line-height);
    font-weight: var(--text-role-label-font-weight);
    letter-spacing: var(--text-role-label-letter-spacing);
    color: var(--review-card-attribution-foreground);
  }

  /* display: contents keeps the wrapper out of layout entirely, so .review-card stays the grid
     item / carousel slide child it was before the dialog existed. */
  .review-card-dialog {
    display: contents;
  }

  .review-card__clamp {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* The whole card opens the dialog (see assets/mionas-review-clamp.js), so it needs to look
     clickable -- but only when there is more to read. [data-overflowing] is set by that same
     element, so the cursor and the behaviour can never disagree. */
  .review-card:has(.review-card__clamp[data-overflowing]) {
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .review-card__quote--clamped {
    display: -webkit-box;
    -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
    -webkit-line-clamp: var(--review-card-quote-clamp, 5);
    line-clamp: var(--review-card-quote-clamp, 5);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* Hidden until assets/mionas-review-clamp.js measures real overflow. Liquid cannot know
     whether the clamped text is actually cut, so this must not default to visible. */
  .review-card__more {
    display: none;
    align-self: flex-start;
    padding: 0;
    background: none;
    border: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    text-align: start;
    font-family: var(--link-base-font-family);
    font-weight: var(--link-base-font-weight);
    font-size: var(--link-size-sm-font-size);
    color: var(--link-base-foreground);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .review-card__clamp[data-overflowing] .review-card__more {
    display: inline-block;
  }

  .review-card__more:hover,
  .review-card__more:focus-visible {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .review-card-dialog__panel {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--card-base-background);
    padding: var(--space-lg);
  }

  /* Scoped to [open]: the UA stylesheet hides a closed <dialog> with
     `dialog:not([open]) { display: none }`. Setting `display` on this selector
     unconditionally would override that rule and leak every closed dialog into the page
     as an inline flex box. `[open]` (not `:modal`) matches what the UA rule keys on and
     what assets/base.css's own `.dialog-modal[open]` already uses. */
  .review-card-dialog__panel[open] {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  /* The body takes initial focus (see tabindex="-1" + autofocus above) purely so the close button
     does not. It is a container, not a control, so it draws no ring of its own -- every real
     control inside the dialog keeps its focus indicator. */
  .review-card-dialog__body:focus {
    outline: none;
  }

  /* Below 750px .dialog-modal (assets/base.css) already goes full-screen -- 100dvh/100dvw with
     its own padding -- which is the right shape for a review on a phone. Only the desktop panel
     needs a cap and its own card chrome. The panel itself never scrolls at any viewport -- see
     .review-card-dialog__body -- so no overflow-y here. */
  @media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
    .review-card-dialog__panel {
      border-radius: var(--card-base-radius);
      box-shadow: var(--card-base-shadow);
      max-height: 80svh;
    }

    /* Vertically centre the panel instead of letting it sit flush against the top of the
       viewport. `!important` is load-bearing and not laziness: the theme's shared
       `.dialog-modal[open]` animation is `elementSlideInTop`, whose final keyframe sets
       `margin-top: 0` with `animation-fill-mode: forwards` (assets/base.css:1804-1814). An
       animation's filled value beats a normal author declaration, so a plain `margin-block: auto`
       is overridden and the dialog stays pinned to the top. Only an important declaration wins
       against it. Scoped to >=750px because below that .dialog-modal is deliberately full-screen
       (100dvh/100dvw), where centring is meaningless. */
    .review-card-dialog__panel[open] {
      margin-block: auto !important;
    }
  }

  .review-card-dialog__body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    overflow-y: auto;
    /* A flex item's default min-height: auto would let this item's content force the item
       (and its ancestor flex containers) to grow instead of scrolling internally. min-height: 0
       lets it actually shrink to the panel's cap so overflow-y: auto has room to engage. */
    min-height: 0;
    /* Room for the absolutely-positioned close button. */
    padding-inline-end: var(--minimum-touch-target);
  }

  .review-card-dialog__close {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-start: var(--space-sm);
    inset-inline-end: var(--space-sm);
  }

  /* The shared .close-button box is already --minimum-touch-target (44px), which is the right
     hit area; only the glyph inside it is small (--icon-size-xs, 0.85rem). Scale just the icon so
     the control reads clearly against a full-width block of review text, without changing the
     touch target or the button's alignment. Scoped to this dialog -- the theme's other close
     buttons sit over imagery and drawers where the smaller glyph suits them. */
  .review-card-dialog__close svg {
    width: var(--icon-size-sm);
    height: var(--icon-size-sm);
  }
{% endstylesheet %}
